Z||000002||Oreif||10-25-2000||01:55 AM||||I personally would go with a platnum plug. If you start getting into brands, tempers seem to flare. So I would suggest get the brand you are comfortable with and get the platnum. Most brands make platnum plugs now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ur2biteme||10-25-2000||05:12 AM||||i have the 87 with DIS.  cant keep platinum plugs in my car.  i run bosch's cheapo plugs, but have ran delcos and autolites with the same performance- good.  if you run the hei, platinum will probably work better.  just havent had much luck with it. (or transmissions for that matter)<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000005||overlord||10-25-2000||07:56 AM||||in my 84 2.5...i used to run el cheapos....then upgreaded to Bosche platinum.....work well for me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||10-25-2000||08:50 AM||||I've been perfectly happy with OEM AC Delco's in my 88 Coupe (and in my 84 Sport Coupe before that).  They're usually good for 35K miles or so.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||10:11 AM||||I am happy with splitfire. I have them in all my cars but they are expensive.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||10-25-2000||11:46 AM||||I use the good old regular ACDelco plugs in my 2.5  "light bulb goes on"  Man I need to get some HUGE ACDelco stickers for my Fiero, that'll show dem ricer's  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> j/k<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||kslish||10-25-2000||12:15 PM||||I personally use AC/Delco Rapidfires in all my cars.  But that is just a personal preference because of a number of reasons:<P>1.) Good design (in my opinion at least).<P>2.) Throttle response seems to be a little better with them in. (purely seat of the pants here)  Seemed to help my '86 4-cyl out more here than my GT, but the GT revs nicely also.<P>3.) It's an AC/Delco product in a GM car.<P>4.) They are a coated plug (nickel I think) so they won't seize up in the plug hole from rusting like your basic cheap steel spark plug.  Makes changing those back plugs on the Fiero V6 easier the next time around.<P>HOWEVER, you do NOT want to use single sided platinum plugs (like your basic Bosch Platinum plugs) in a car with DIS ignition like the '87-'88 4-cyl Fieros.  The reverse "waste spark" (DIS fires the plugs in sets of two) will rapidly erode the platinum center electrode and render the plug worthless within a few thousand miles.  I think Double-tipped platinums (I've seen them made by Autolite) solves this problem, but haven't heard first hand about them from anyone with experience with them in Fieros.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||05:20 PM||||I forgot to mention that I have had nothing but bad luck with autolite. I wouldn't even use them in my lawnmower if it was all they made!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||10-25-2000||07:47 PM||||Odd. I've never once had a problem with an autolite plug in over 20 years. That's what's in my DIS motor now.<P>Autolite's platinum plug, at least the ones I put in my van, is a traditional design plug with a platinum gob stuck on the center electrode. They work fine in the van. I don't know if I'd try them in my DIS motor or not.||||reg||1
Z||000020||webbee||10-25-2000||08:13 PM||||Not the "sparkplug thread" again. <P>Gentlepeople lets be civilized...<P>I use AC in the 2m4.<P>Change at 10k miles or once a year which<BR>ever occurs first.||||reg||1
